    "faq_strong_girl": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_airfryer": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "No air fryer &mdash; can I make this in the oven?",
            "text": "<p>Yes. Roast the sweet potatoes at <strong>425&deg;F for 25&ndash;30 minutes</strong>, tossing once, and give the salmon <strong>10&ndash;12 minutes at 400&deg;F</strong> on a sheet pan. The air fryer is faster and gets the potato edges crisper, but nothing here depends on it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_temp": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the salmon is done?",
            "text": "<p>Pull it at about <strong>130&deg;F</strong> at the thickest point and let it rest 3&ndash;5 minutes &mdash; carryover heat finishes it around 145&deg;F. King Salmon is fatty enough to stay forgiving, but 8&ndash;10 minutes at 400&deg;F is genuinely the window. Our full <a href=\"/pages/the-ultimate-guide-to-salmon-internal-temp\">guide to salmon internal temp</a> has the detail.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_sweetpotato": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if I can&rsquo;t find Japanese sweet potatoes?",
            "text": "<p>Regular orange sweet potato works, but it holds more moisture &mdash; cut the dice slightly larger and expect softer edges rather than crisp ones. Japanese sweet potato (purple skin, pale flesh) is drier and sweeter, which is why it air fries so well.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_sauce": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long does the herby green sauce keep?",
            "text": "<p>Three to four days refrigerated in a sealed jar. It darkens after day one because the herbs oxidize &mdash; that is cosmetic, not spoilage. A thin film of olive oil on top slows it down.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_avocado": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The assembly step mentions avocado but it is not in the ingredient list.",
            "text": "<p>Correct &mdash; add <strong>half an avocado per bowl</strong>, sliced, at assembly. It is optional but it rounds out the fat and makes the bowl considerably more filling.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_defrost": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I defrost Seatopia King Salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator, wrapped in paper, is best for texture. In a hurry, drop the vacuum-sealed portion into a bowl of cold water for 30&ndash;60 minutes. Never at room temperature or in the microwave.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
